<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Optional chapter in the Loops unit. Adapted from FOPP "The for Loop"
     (Python's for-in) and "Lists and for loops". GNU FDL 1.3; see for-loops/ch-loops.ptx. -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-for-of">
  <title>Optional: The <c>for...of</c> Loop</title>
  <introduction>

    <p>
      When you loop over an array, you often only care about the elements, not their indexes.
      JavaScript has a shorter loop for that, called <c>for...of</c>.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-forof-1">
      <title>Invitations with <c>for...of</c></title>
      <program xml:id="ac-forof-1" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
  let friends = ["Joe", "Amy", "Brad", "Zuki"];

  for (const friend of friends) {
    console.log("Hi " + friend + ", please come to my party on Saturday!");
  }
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      This does the same thing as the counting loop from <xref ref="sec-arrays-by-index"/>.
      Here is how it works:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p><c>friends</c> is the array to loop over.</p></li>
      <li><p><c>friend</c> is the loop variable. On each pass, it holds the next <em>element</em>, not an index. First <c>"Joe"</c>, then <c>"Amy"</c>, and so on.</p></li>
      <li><p>There is no start, condition, or update to write. The loop runs once per element and stops by itself.</p></li>
      <li><p>We can use <c>const</c> here, because each pass gets a brand new <c>friend</c> variable.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-for-of-which">
    <title>Which Loop Should You Use?</title>

    <p>
      Use <c>for...of</c> when you only need to read each element.
      Use the counting <c>for</c> loop when you need any of these:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><p>the index itself (for example, to print <q>Item 3 of 10</q>)</p></li>
      <li><p>to <em>change</em> the elements of the array, since <c>array[i] = ...</c> needs <c>i</c></p></li>
      <li><p>to count something that is not an array, like 1 to 100</p></li>
      <li><p>to count down, or to skip elements</p></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Changing the loop variable in a <c>for...of</c> loop does <em>not</em> change the array.
      Try it below: the array is the same after the loop.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-forof-2">
      <title>Changing the Loop Variable</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-forof-2" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
let prices = [4, 10, 7];

for (let price of prices) {
  price = price + 1;
}

console.log(prices);
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      That is why the tests in this course, and most of the code quizzes, use the counting <c>for</c> loop.
      Make sure you know that one first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-for-of-naming">
    <title>Naming the Loop Variable</title>

    <p>
      In a <c>for...of</c> loop, name the loop variable with the singular of the array name:
      <c>for (const song of songs)</c>, <c>for (const score of scores)</c>.
      Each pass, the variable holds one song or one score, so the name should say that.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-for-of-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-forof-value" label="mc-forof-value">
      <statement>
        <p>On the second pass of this loop, what is the value of <c>pet</c>?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  let pets = ["dog", "cat", "fish"];

  for (const pet of pets) {
    console.log(pet);
  }
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>"cat"</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. A <c>for...of</c> loop variable holds an element, and the second element is <c>"cat"</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>1</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. A <c>for...of</c> loop variable holds an element, not an index.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>"dog"</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. That is the first pass.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>["dog", "cat", "fish"]</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. That is the whole array. The loop variable holds one element at a time.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-forof-when" label="mc-forof-when">
      <statement>
        <p>You need to add 10 points to every score in an array. Which loop do you need?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>A counting <c>for</c> loop, so you can write <c>scores[i] = scores[i] + 10</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. Changing an element needs its index.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>A <c>for...of</c> loop, so you can write <c>score = score + 10</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. That changes the loop variable, but not the array.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
